},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"', and adding "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" will improve the answer for these endpoints only if this inequality holds. Having this, we need to consider only "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/5a8a123241df218136e025dcb503180a1256ec4245b641e1f9e701843ae51d5c.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" values of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The asymptotic of the solution is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/f8248ba86f72f333051b23ad80f483fef65c86a2c74bac866575a210697bd20a.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"765G"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/765/problem/G"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"765G - МАТЕМАТNКА"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"First I'll describe our original approach to this problem, and then some issues we encountered after the round finished."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Suppose that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 2. If we have "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/0dd6da894de1543e52a73e1e3425a74e11b6cb82a861a912a1f40aa0a3e6561f.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", then the string "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" must look like 0?0?0?..., that is, all even positions 0, 2, 4, ... will have 0's (for all others, we can't say), and if "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/d16f2b27f59c88bf065aebb2d8a654b7f013d62feb5a0a309218faa3be78b239.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", then "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" looks like ?0?0?0.... Similarly, knowing the remainder of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" modulo a prime "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in factorization of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"N"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" implies that there are zeros in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" positions apart. If we fix all remainder modulo "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", ..., "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", then the string will be determined unambigiously. Notice that after fixing the modulos, there are still "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"N"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"' = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"α"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"... "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"α"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" possible "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"'s between 0 and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"N"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1; we will simply multiply the answer by "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"N"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"' in the end."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"At this point we have a brute-force solution: try all values for remainders, count how many of the values give the string "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". This is of course too slow, but we can make a few optimizations:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":false},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" Make a brute-force into a DP by introducing memoization. We will count the number of ways to obtain each partial string "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" after fixing first "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" remainders. Of course, we don't have to store all 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" masks, but just the reachable ones."}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" Once "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", each choice of remainder either places a single zero into a string, or doesn't change anything. At this point we are not interested in the full mask, but rather in the number of \"unsatisfied\" zeros of initial string "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Each transition either satisfies one zero, or doesn't change anything; the number of transitions of each kind is easy to count."}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We will do a full memoization DP for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", and continue with a compressed DP once "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". The second part can be done in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nm"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") time and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") memory. The complexity of the first part depends on the total number of states in the memoization DP."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Turns out this number can be much larger than we anticipated on certain tests, for instance, primes starting from 5 or 7. On these cases, all our model solutions received time out. Such tests didn't appear in the original test set, of course."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"[user:KAN] and myself tried to improve the solution. The idea behind our optimization is that once "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" / 2, several central bits of the mask behave just like the \"unsatisfied\" bits in the large-prime part of the original solution: if we choose to cover them, it will be the single bit we cover. Thus we can do a \"hybrid\" DP that has parameters (number of unsatisfied bits in the middle, mask of all the rest bits). "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"[user:KAN]'s solution used \"naive\" mask DP for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ 23, switched to static array for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 29, 31, 37, and then proceeded to large primes as before. I tried to write a self-adaptive solution that handles all ranges of primes pretty much the same way. [user:KAN] was more successful: his solution works in "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/54051ade6a7060c75dee3dd0f48c50de6206a6573f978503c1625d1d5201087d.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" seconds on all cases we could counstruct; my solution works in "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/068aa81a2e81ed0988756c3f8062fefdef06e1e6ea259e45d10a6102ffedbcec.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" seconds and uses a lot of excess memory."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"[user:KAN]'s solution: [submission:24707930], my solution: [submission:24707929]."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/50456"}